WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:05.760
Why didn't Rhaenys roast the Greens? Will Laenor&nbsp;
ever row back home? "House of the Dragon" Season&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:10.080
1 left fans wanting more — with several burning&nbsp;
questions still lingering after the finale.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:14.700
Though he's conflict avoidant, frequently&nbsp;
ill, and largely ineffectual as a leader,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:19.740
much of the story in "House of the Dragon" Season&nbsp;
1 still revolves around Viserys Targaryen. He is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:24.240
to be fair, quite an interesting character —&nbsp;
a man wholly unqualified to keep war at bay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:27.360
but who remains the only one in a&nbsp;
real position to maintain peace.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:32.160
Is he a hero? Certainly not. He let his&nbsp;
delusions of foresight and his desire for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:37.500
greatness push his wife to her deathbed. Is he a&nbsp;
villain? Not necessarily. He does keep the peace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:41.940
after all, and he stays loyal to Rhaenyra&nbsp;
until the very end. Viserys is undoubtedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:44.580
a sympathetic figure, but that&nbsp;
doesn't make him a great king.

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:48.840
Repeatedly throughout "House of the Dragon"&nbsp;
Season 1, we get scenes debating this question:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:52.980
What does it mean to be a good king? What&nbsp;
does it mean to leave a lasting legacy?

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:57.180
"What will they say of me when&nbsp;
the histories are written?"

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:01.740
A lover of history, Viserys knows that his reign&nbsp;
will never become a great song. In the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:05.580
he only wishes for the feuds within his own&nbsp;
house to be settled before his death. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:08.940
that doesn't happen. Is Viserys the&nbsp;
king who held the peace, or the king&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:13.500
whose inaction led to widespread bloodshed?&nbsp;
Ultimately, that's up to the viewer to decide.

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:17.940
Princess Rhaenys Targaryen plays a unique&nbsp;
role in the story of "House of the Dragon."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:22.020
As the so-called "Queen Who Never Was,"&nbsp;
she represents an alternate path that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:25.740
Westeros could have gone down. Had she been&nbsp;
given the Iron Throne instead of Viserys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:28.380
things might have gone quite&nbsp;
differently for everyone involved.

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:31.260
She clearly would have been a&nbsp;
stronger and more decisive ruler,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:35.100
and she would have set the precedent that&nbsp;
women can reign as well as men. Such a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:38.760
precedent surely would have prevented the kind&nbsp;
of schism that takes place in the show, right?

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:43.980
Well, that's hard to say. The truth is, even if&nbsp;
Rhaenys had been selected as queen, she probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:47.640
would have faced the same kinds of challenges&nbsp;
that Rhaenyra faces throughout Season 1.

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:54.480
"Men would sooner put the realm to the torch&nbsp;
than see a woman ascend the Iron Throne."

00:01:55.260 --> 00:02:00.120
Had she been named Jaehaerys' heir, Westeros might&nbsp;
have fallen into chaos even sooner. In the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:02.640
though, the great council may have&nbsp;
only prolonged the inevitable.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:07.740
Whether or not Rhaenys could have saved the realm&nbsp;
from war as queen, she has a pretty clear chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:12.090
to stop further bloodshed when she escapes&nbsp;
on Meleys from the Dragonpit. In other words,

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:12.590
"There is a beast beneath the boards!"

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:19.080
After bursting through the floor on her&nbsp;
roaring mount, Rhaenys finds herself staring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:24.120
down Rhaenyra's entire rival faction — Otto&nbsp;
Hightower, Ser Criston Cole, Alicent, and all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:28.200
three of her children. With a single "dracarys",&nbsp;
Rhaenys could have burned the lot of them and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:32.880
saved the realm from the conflict that now seems&nbsp;
inevitable. But she refuses and simply flies away.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:37.680
Why does Rhaenys show mercy at this moment when&nbsp;
she already plans to ally herself with Rhaenyra?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:42.720
There are a few possible explanations. While&nbsp;
she disagrees with Alicent's conviction that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:46.500
women must remain in the background, Rhaenys&nbsp;
clearly has sympathy for the Green Queen.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:50.580
Also, Rhaenys might not have fully&nbsp;
chosen a side yet. Seeing Rhaenyra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:53.520
with her crown and loyal followers is&nbsp;
a powerful moment for the princess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:56.880
and it might have taken that extra&nbsp;
push to convince her to take up arms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:00.960
Killing a whole royal family without&nbsp;
a plan isn't necessarily a great call.

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:04.680
One thing that's for sure is that Rhaenys&nbsp;
didn't spare the Greens simply because she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:09.720
abhors violence. After all, she must have killed&nbsp;
countless innocents while escaping the Dragonpit.

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:14.400
Vhagar is the largest living dragon in the world,&nbsp;
and in the "House of the Dragon" Season 1 finale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:17.940
she shows just how terrifying and&nbsp;
dangerous she can be. With a single bite,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:22.680
she rips Arrax and the young Prince Lucerys&nbsp;
to pieces, establishing herself as being in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:25.260
an entirely different league from&nbsp;
many of the show's other dragons.

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:30.180
Sure, Caraxes, Syrax, and Meleys are all&nbsp;
fearsome dragons, but it might take all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:34.020
three of them flying in tandem to even have&nbsp;
a shot at taking Vhagar down. We're talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:38.340
about a dragon who survived Aegon's conquest and&nbsp;
is still just as deadly over a century later.

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:42.900
Fortunately for the Blacks, Daemon is already&nbsp;
hard at work recruiting the remaining riderless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:46.800
dragons to their cause, beginning with&nbsp;
the next largest beast after Vhagar.

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:53.340
"Dragonstone has 13 to their 4. I also have a&nbsp;
score of eggs incubating in the Dragonmont."

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:59.160
During the final episode of "House of the Dragon"&nbsp;
Season 1, Daemon Targaryen journeys deep into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:03.720
Dragonmont — the volcano of Dragonstone — in&nbsp;
search of a new ally. Knowing that the coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:07.140
war between the Blacks and the Greens will&nbsp;
likely come down to their respective dragons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:10.800
he aims to recruit the riderless dragons&nbsp;
who nest on the island to his cause.

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:14.280
We see him approaching the lair of the&nbsp;
old dragon Vermithor while singing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:18.000
Valyrian tune, and the dragon seems&nbsp;
to respond quite positively to it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.240
belching fire throughout the&nbsp;
cavern but leaving Daemon unburnt.

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:24.960
Establishing contact with Vermithor is&nbsp;
great for the Blacks, especially since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:28.740
the creature is one of the only dragons who&nbsp;
might be able to challenge Vhagar. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:32.340
there's one big question that no one knows&nbsp;
the answer to yet: Who will ride him?

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:36.300
The only person in Rhaenyra's faction&nbsp;
with Targaryen blood and no Dragon is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:39.960
Daemon's young daughter Rhaena, who&nbsp;
could be a suitable match. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:43.200
it's also possible that a&nbsp;
non-Targaryen could claim Vermithor.

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:47.340
Rhaenyra's relationship with her uncle&nbsp;
Daemon is complicated, to say the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:50.760
absolute least. Though she's repeatedly&nbsp;
shown pursuing him throughout her life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:54.720
and though incestuous relationships are clearly&nbsp;
not that taboo in the world of the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:58.200
it's still hard not to see their&nbsp;
early dynamic as deeply problematic.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:02.100
At best, she chooses to be with a man who&nbsp;
killed his first wife in order to advance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:06.420
his own station. At worst, she was groomed&nbsp;
from childhood to love a deeply dangerous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:10.140
and treacherous person. Rhaenyra is&nbsp;
too smart not to understand the kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:14.220
of person Daemon is — a person whose only&nbsp;
real interest is his gaining more power.

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:18.180
However, she seems to believe that, for&nbsp;
some reason, she's the one person who's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:23.280
impervious to his dark side. She's different from&nbsp;
Mysaria and Lady Rhea Royce; from her own father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:27.420
who Daemon constantly fought with. For&nbsp;
whatever reason — be it ego or blindness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:31.200
caused by "love" — she seems to think he's a&nbsp;
weapon that could never be turned against her.

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:35.640
In the Season 1 finale, however, we see&nbsp;
that this isn't true. Daemon shows a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:38.760
viciousness toward Rhaenyra when she&nbsp;
starts to question his warmongering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:42.120
and though he eventually defers to her&nbsp;
authority, it's clear that he has his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:46.260
own intentions. Can Rhaenyra really trust him&nbsp;
to have her back when things start to heat up?

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:52.440
"Dreams didn't make us kings. Dragons did."

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:55.920
One of the larger mysteries in "House of&nbsp;
the Dragon" Season 1 is what becomes of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.920 --> 00:06:00.420
Laenor Velaryon. Rhaenyra Targaryen's first&nbsp;
husband is believed by most of the realm to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:04.500
be dead after a fight with his lover Qarl,&nbsp;
but that's far from the truth. In reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:08.220
Laenor faked his death, shaved&nbsp;
his unmistakable Valyrian] locks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:12.780
and set off for parts unknown with Qarl and a&nbsp;
boatload of money courtesy of Daemon and Rhaenyra.

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:15.900
The implication is that the two&nbsp;
Targaryens never intended to kill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:19.980
Laenor, as faking his death serves multiple&nbsp;
purposes. It allows him to set off on a life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:23.760
of adventure with his real love, it&nbsp;
allows Rhaenyra and Daemon to marry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:28.020
and it makes the realm fear Rhaenyra a bit more&nbsp;
via the rumor that she had her husband murdered.

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:31.620
That's all confusing enough, and it doesn't&nbsp;
even touch on the matter of where Laenor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:36.060
might be. In all likelihood, he's partying&nbsp;
it up with Qarl in some far-flung corner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:40.020
of the Free Cities. But what if something&nbsp;
happens? What if he and Qarl break up and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:43.860
the good knight decides to reveal the truth&nbsp;
to the world? It's possible that the former&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:47.880
prince is out of the story for good, but he&nbsp;
could return under a number of circumstances.

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:52.380
An elusive-yet-powerful "House of&nbsp;
the Dragon" character is Mysaria,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:57.540
the King's Landing madam who sets up a spy network&nbsp;
within the Red Keep itself. Though she lingers in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:01.680
the background for much of Season 1, Mysaria&nbsp;
is always present, and she steps back into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:06.300
the spotlight late in the season to reveal the&nbsp;
location of Prince Aegon upon King Viserys' death.

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:11.460
As Lord Larys Strong has a spy network of his&nbsp;
own, he sees Mysaria as a dangerous rival and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:15.360
moves to eliminate her at the end of Season&nbsp;
1. We see an agent of Larys' walking away from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:19.980
Mysaria's burning brothel, presumably signaling&nbsp;
her death. But is she actually out of the picture?

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:24.360
Surely someone with so much power in King's&nbsp;
Landing can't be relegated to an off-screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:28.200
death. Mysaria has a whole system of&nbsp;
agents and loyalists, and it's likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:32.760
that she would have known about Lord Larys'&nbsp;
attack ahead of time. If Mysaria is alive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:36.240
she may not show her face any time&nbsp;
soon — she's too smart for that.

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:42.180
Alicent Hightower is a curious case. At times&nbsp;
both a tragic character and a vile villain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:45.960
she stands opposite Rhaenyra at nearly&nbsp;
every turn. While it's easy to condemn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:49.560
her for following her father's power-hungry&nbsp;
schemes and defending her sons during their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:52.800
many egregious crimes, Alicent&nbsp;
isn't such a simple character.

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:56.940
From the time she was a child, her father&nbsp;
pushed her into intimacy with King Viserys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.940 --> 00:08:00.240
knowing full well that she might&nbsp;
become his queen. Alicent hardly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:03.300
had a say in this decision, and once&nbsp;
she starts bearing the king's children,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:06.720
her sense of duty to her family prevents&nbsp;
her from even considering escape.

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:10.020
With her own son Aemond now the&nbsp;
murderer of Rhaenyra's son Lucerys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:13.680
it seems unlikely that olive branches&nbsp;
will ever be extended. And yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:18.480
it's still easy to see the regret in Alicent's&nbsp;
eyes. Her closest allies are Larys Strong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:22.500
who she seems to pay by gratifying&nbsp;
his foot fetish, and Ser Criston Cole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:27.060
who's proven to be a violent, jealous, and&nbsp;
unpredictable man. Perhaps Rhaenys is right.

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:32.700
"You desire not to be free, but to build&nbsp;
a window in the wall of your prison."

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:38.100
Perhaps the Dowager Queen really is too far gone&nbsp;
to ever find a life of real happiness. But maybe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:41.760
just maybe, Alicent Hightower still&nbsp;
has some surprises up her sleeve.

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:46.680
Like her father before her, Rhaenyra is obsessed&nbsp;
with the prophetic Song of Ice and Fire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:51.180
Aegon the Conqueror's dream foreseeing the&nbsp;
invasion of Westeros by the White Walkers.

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:59.820
"From my blood, come the Prince That Was Promised,&nbsp;
and his will be the Song of Ice and Fire."

00:09:00.660 --> 00:09:04.800
The prophecy plays a huge part in "House&nbsp;
of the Dragon,", as it gives Rhaenyra a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:08.640
big-picture reason to believe she must be&nbsp;
the one to sit the Iron Throne. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:11.160
foresight is rarely a simple thing in fantasy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:15.540
and it remains to be seen exactly how the Song of&nbsp;
Ice and Fire will play into the coming conflict.

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:20.340
"House of the Dragon" would seem to be set in the&nbsp;
same canon as its predecessor, as it even shares&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:24.540
the same theme song. But in "Game of Thrones," the&nbsp;
Targaryen prophecy doesn't really play out the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:29.760
you'd think. Arya Stark is the one who kills the&nbsp;
Night King, Jon Snow proves largely ineffectual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:34.740
(even after being brought back from the dead), and&nbsp;
Daenerys goes crazy and burns down King's Landing.

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:38.700
A prophecy that plays out so chaotically&nbsp;
hardly seems like a good reason to put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:42.600
countless innocent lives at risk, but it&nbsp;
may be the final push Rhaenyra needs to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:46.740
declare war after her son's death. Of course,&nbsp;
Daemon doesn't even believe in the prophecy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:50.400
and it's possible that it may be lost to&nbsp;
history amidst all the fighting to come.

